# Count Bernadotte
Tamil title: கவுண்ட் பெர்னாதத்

Jewish men, women, and children were subjected to Nazi torture. The credit for taking nearly seven thousand Jewish women from those two torture prisons to Sweden without danger to their lives belongs to Count Bernadotte, the United Nations mediator who was shot dead in the territory of Israel a few days ago by some Jewish extremists. That some among the very Jews whom this able and benevolent man had rescued from Nazi cruelty and imminent execution should shoot him dead is an event that must shock anyone.

Bernadotte descended from the French royal line and had settled in Sweden. He studied many branches of military art at the Karlberg military academy and distinguished himself as an officer in the Swedish cavalry. The Red Cross division attached to Sweden’s air-defence service operated under his supervision.

It was during the Second World War that a fitting opportunity arose for his service. In April 1945 British fighter aircraft attacked Lübeck on the Baltic coast. The shipyard and railway station were levelled. At midnight the sirens announced that the danger of air attack had passed. In a small mansion near the city stayed Himmler, Germany’s leader. Bernadotte was with him, discussing the future of the European peoples with great concern.

During the war Sweden’s position became extremely difficult. At home, some supported the Nazis and others the Allied nations. Bernadotte accepted responsibility for lovingly protecting the thousands of refugees who came by sea from Finland and the Baltic regions, and discharged it efficiently.

In November 1943 Allied aircraft attacked Berlin heavily. Bernadotte was there even then. On another occasion, without fear for his life, he had to hide in a sewer pit to protect himself from an air raid.

To negotiate with men such as Himmler, avoid the traps they laid, and successfully obtain only what one sought was not something an ordinary person could readily accomplish. It required extraordinary ability, a skilful tongue that gave nothing away, and deep firmness of purpose. In these circumstances, for the people of Europe generally and especially...

---NOTES---
The second scan ends in the middle of the final sentence. The Tamil form பெர்னாதத் is retained in the Tamil title; the standard English spelling is Bernadotte.
